## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of process lifecycle, waitpid usage, and zombie avoidance in a practical multi-child scenario.

## Key Concepts
- waitpid semantics and looping
- SIGCHLD handling and reaping
- WNOHANG and non-blocking variants
- Zombie processes and edge cases (stopped/continued)

## Code Example
```javascript
#include <sys/types.h>
#include <sys/wait.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <errno.h>

int main() {
  // spawn N children ...
  int finished = 0; int status;
  while (finished < N) {
    pid_t pid = waitpid(-1, &status, 0);
    if (pid > 0) { finished++; /* process status */ }
    else if (pid == -1 && errno == ECHILD) break;
  }
  return 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for SIGCHLD storms or large N?
- What are the risks of ignoring zombie cleanup in long-running daemons?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical I/O scheduling knowledge, correctness, and minimal code for a common OS component.

## Key Concepts
- SCAN elevator algorithm
- Head movement and direction reversal
- Service order derivation from a head position
- Edge cases: empty queue, all requests on one side

## Code Example
```c
// toy SCAN scheduler (upward)
int scan_schedule(int head, int req[], int n, int out[]) {
  int up[n], dn[n], upn=0, dnn=0, k=0;
  for (int i=0;i<n;i++) if (req[i] >= head) up[upn++] = req[i]; else dn[dnn++] = req[i];
  qsort(up, upn, sizeof(int), cmp_asc);
  qsort(dn, dnn, sizeof(int), cmp_desc);
  for (int i=0;i<upn;i++) out[k++] = up[i];
  for (int i=0;i<dnn;i++) out[k++] = dn[i];
  return k; // number of entries
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to adapt SCAN for multiple disks or priority requests?
- How would you test correctness with random traces and measure average seek distance?
